 See [MEMORY_ACCESS.md](MEMORY_ACCESS.md) for the hypothesis and comparison results.
+
+## Step 7 size and vector sweeps
+
+`--suite baseline` (the default) selects the original seven shapes, even though
+the executable also registers the expanded cases. `--suite memory-access` selects
+24 shapes: both vector orientations at sizes 64, 128, 255, 256, 257, 512, 1024,
+and (32,256,N) for N = 1, 4, 16, 17, 64, 255, 256, 257, 512, 1024.
+The N=17 case checks the scalar/vector transition identified in compiler inspection.
+The collector uses an exact anchored filter and validates the selected suite only.
+This keeps the original baseline independently reproducible and avoids duplicate
+registrations for vector size 256. The two families are analyzed separately.
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/run_benchmarks.py --suite memory-access \
+  --warmup 1 --min-time 1 --repetitions 10 --prevent-idle-sleep \
+  --implementation-order reference-first --notes "Actual observed conditions"
+python3 scripts/run_benchmarks.py --suite memory-access \
+  --warmup 1 --min-time 1 --repetitions 10 --prevent-idle-sleep \
+  --implementation-order ikj-first --notes "Actual observed conditions"
+```
+
+Each expanded capture retains 480 individual measurements. The summarizer adds
+`storage_bytes = 4*(M*K + K*N + M*N)` and `reference_B_stride_bytes = 4*N`
+while retaining CPU/real timing and variability. Storage is an allocation footprint,
+not an assertion that every byte is active in a particular cache.
